      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "In many organisms in which the population structure is hierarchical, it is useful to be able to calculate directly the average genotype frequencies across all subpopulations. Equations 6.12 through 6.14 make it possible to deduce the average genotype frequencies. To do this, first note that $ R_{fused} $ in Equation 6.12 equals $ \\overline{q}^2 $ and $ P_{fused} $ in Equation 6.13 equals $ \\overline{p}^2 $. Hence Equation 6.12 implies the average genotype frequency of aa among the subpopulations is given by $ \\sigma_q^2 + \\bar{p} \\bar{q} $, and Equation 6.13 implies the average genotype frequency of AA among the subpopulations is given by $ \\sigma_p^2 + \\bar{p} \\bar{q} $. Since there are only two alleles, $ \\sigma_q^2 = \\sigma_p^2 = \\sigma^2 $, and Equation 6.14 says that $ \\sigma^2 = F_{\\mathrm{ST}} \\times \\bar{p} \\times \\bar{q} $. Putting all this together, the average genotype frequency of AA across subpopulations must equal $ \\bar{p}^2 + F_{\\mathrm{ST}} \\bar{p} \\bar{q} $, and the average genotype frequency of aa across subpopulations must equal $ \\bar{q}^2 + F_{\\mathrm{ST}} \\bar{p} \\bar{q} $. Because every genotype that is not homozygous must be heterozygous, the average genotype frequency of heterozygotes across subpopulations is given by $ 1 - (\\overline{p}^2 + F_{ST} \\overline{p} \\overline{q}) - (\\overline{q}^2 + F_{ST} \\overline{p} \\overline{q}) $. Note that $ 1 - \\overline{p}^2 - \\overline{q}^2 = 2 \\overline{p} \\overline{q} $, and so the average frequency of heterozygotes simplifies to $ 2 \\overline{p} \\overline{q} - 2 \\overline{p} \\overline{q} F_{ST} $.",
